_model: other_work
---
title: How this site is built
---
order: 6
---
body:

This edition is a static site built with [Lektor](https://www.getlektor.com/).
Every page is generated ahead of time from plain text files, so nothing is
queried when a proposition is opened — the browser receives finished HTML, and
the only code that runs is the diagram library.

This page describes how it is put together, and why it is put together that way,
for anyone who wants to build something similar: a structured text whose parts
are addressed individually and whose figures are live.

### The problem the structure solves

A proposition is not one piece of prose. It has a statement, a proof, marginal
justifications for the proof's steps, one or more figures, and commentary about
the whole. Those parts are read differently and used differently. The proof is
stepped through; the commentary stays put beside it. The statement is quoted by
other pages. A justification points at the postulate it leans on.

If the page is written as one blob of markup, none of that is available: the
parts exist only typographically, and any interaction has to re-find them by
guessing at the HTML. So the first decision is that the content model names the
parts, and the template assembles them. Everything interactive on this site
follows from that one choice.

### The project

```
euclids-elements.lektorproject   project config and publish targets
models/            one .ini per content type — the fields a page of that kind has
flowblocks/        repeatable sub-sections a page can hold any number of
templates/         one .html per model, plus the shared layout
content/           the pages themselves, as contents.lr text files
packages/          local plugins
assets/            css, js, images, copied through to the output
scripts/           build-time checks
```

**`models/`** is where the shape of a page is declared. There is a model per
kind of thing the text contains — proposition, definition, postulate, common
notion, book, essay — rather than one generic page model, because they differ in
what they must carry and in how they are cited. A postulate has no proof; a book
has a table of contents assembled from its children.

**`flowblocks/`** covers the cases where a page holds a variable number of
something. Not every proposition is just a proposition: some carry a lemma
before, or a corollary after. Modelling the body as a *list of sections* rather
than a fixed proof field means those pages need no special case.

**`templates/`** turn a model's fields into HTML. Because the fields are
separate, the template can wrap each section in its own box, put justifications
in the margin, and render the guide commentary below — decisions that would be
hard-coded into the prose otherwise.

### One page in full

A page is a `contents.lr` file in a directory; the directory path is the URL.
Fields are separated by a line of three dashes, and flow blocks by a `#### name ####` line with `----` between their fields. Proposition I.1 begins:

<pre><code>_model: proposition
&#45;&#45;&#45;
title: Proposition 1
&#45;&#45;&#45;
order: 1
&#45;&#45;&#45;
short_label: I.1
&#45;&#45;&#45;
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To construct an equilateral triangle on a given finite straight line.
----
proof:

Let {AB} be the given finite straight line.

&lt;figure class="diagram"&gt;
&lt;canvas id="canvas_0" width="340" height="260" tabindex="0"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
&lt;script type="text/javascript"&gt;
geomlib.init({
    canvasid: "canvas_0",
    elements: [
        "A;point;free;125,130",
        "B;point;free;215,130",
        "AB;line;connect;A,B",
        ...
    ],
    slides: [ ... ],
});
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;

Describe the circle BCD with centre A and radius AB. [!just I.Post.3]
...
----
guide:
...
</code></pre>

`_model` picks both the field set and the template. `short_label` is the
citation form other pages use. `order` sorts it among its siblings, which is
also what drives the previous/next navigation, so no page needs to know its
neighbours.

Three things in the proof body are worth pointing at, because each is a small
extension rather than plain markdown:

- **`{AB}`** marks an element reference. It becomes a span bound to the figure
  beside it, so hovering the letters in the prose lights the matching element in
  the diagram. Described on the [Using](/geomlib/using/) page.
- **`[!just I.Post.3]`** is a justification. It renders in the margin next to
  the step it supports and links to the postulate it names.
- **The `<figure>`** carries the diagram as an element list. The same list also
  feeds `slides`, so the static figure and the stepped proof are one source of
  truth rather than two descriptions that can disagree.

### Modelling the source, not an idealised version of it

Joyce often wrote several definitions in one file, with a single commentary
covering the group. The tempting move is to flatten that — split them into
independent pages and duplicate the commentary, or merge them into one page and
lose the individual addresses.

Neither is right, so there is a **definition bundle**: each definition gets its
own page and its own citable address, while a hidden bundle page holds the
shared commentary. Nothing is duplicated and nothing loses its address.

This is the general lesson of the conversion. Where the source's structure
disagrees with the structure you would have chosen, model the source. The
awkwardness is real and will resurface later as a broken link if it is papered
over.

### Extending the generator

Three local plugins live in `packages/`, each answering one mismatch between the
text and the tooling.

**Citations and element references.** The prose is dense with cross-references.
Hand-written links would not survive a change of URL scheme, so the source says
`@I.47` and the plugin resolves it. The same plugin builds the reverse index:
the *this proposition is used in…* list at the foot of a page is written nowhere,
but collected from every citation that points at that page. It also renders the
`{AB}` element references.

**Mathematics** is rendered at build time rather than in the browser, so readers
are not sent a maths library for the few pages that need one.

**Roman-numeral URLs** is the smallest and most particular. Book X restarts its
definition numbering in three subsections, so addresses end in fragments like
`defX.III.6`. Python reads the trailing `.6` as an unrecognised file extension,
and the development server offered those pages as downloads instead of
displaying them. Forty lines to say that these are pages.

That last one is the honest shape of most of this work: a text's own numbering
outliving the assumptions of the tools carrying it.

### Checking what the generator cannot

A static build validates its own model, not yours. Lektor will happily produce a
page whose diagram is misspecified — the figure simply does not appear, and the
page still looks finished.

So every figure on the site is exercised before anything is published. Each
page's diagram code is run against the real library in a sandbox, and a page that
throws, or that names an element which does not exist, stops the deploy. The
check paid for itself immediately: it found a Book IV figure whose element
declarations had been transposed during conversion, which had been silently
falling back to a static image ever since it was written.

A second check keeps the library version consistent, because a version written
into prose cannot be templated and will rot without anyone noticing.

### Reusing it

The site is open source at
[github.com/brownnrl/euclids-elements-lektor](https://github.com/brownnrl/euclids-elements-lektor).
The models, flow blocks, templates and plugins are the parts worth lifting; the
content is Joyce's. See [geomlib](/geomlib/) for the diagram library itself, and
the [Quickstart](/geomlib/quickstart/) for putting one figure on a page of your
own.
